Stop building Lean for wasm with pthreads.

Three places make the artifact a pthread build, and all three have to go — removing the CMake
`MULTI_THREAD` option alone would leave two of them, and `-pthread` is what emits atomics and TLS into
every object and forces a shared memory at link time. A shared memory is what needs `SharedArrayBuffer`,
which is what needs cross-origin isolation in the browser (FINDINGS.md §2).

Not changed, deliberately:

  - `-fwasm-exceptions` stays. Native EH is independent of threads, and the JS-emulated alternative
    consumes browser JS stack on every potentially-throwing call, which a deep kernel recursion cannot
    afford (src/CMakeLists.txt l.205-209).
  - `-sSTACK_SIZE=16777216` stays, and matters more here than before: with no worker threads the import
    runs on the main wasm stack, and Emscripten's 64KB default overflows it (l.918-919).
  - `-sALLOW_MEMORY_GROWTH=1` stays, and now actually engages — a shared memory in this configuration
    cannot grow, which is why the worker reserves gigabytes up front and why
    cauli/lean4-wasm-in-browser carries `scripts/patch-wasm-memory.py` to raise the declared maximum by
    hand. An unshared memory grows.
  - `-sMAIN_MODULE=2` and the explicit `emscripten-exports.txt` list stay; they are about `dlsym` and
    glue size, not threads.

See wasm-build/README.md. Variant 1 (the fork's stated direction) additionally configures with
`-DMULTI_THREAD=OFF`, which is a configure-time flag rather than a patch: with the macro undefined the
task manager is compiled out and `IO.Promise.new` panics during the `Init` import.

